`Conditions.evolve` returns immediately before binding `conditions` when `status` is empty or when all requested statuses already match the current object, so those frames contribute no observations. In every other call, the method creates a fresh `conditions = {}` and then, inside the loop over `self.conditions.items()`, assigns one entry per iteration with `conditions[condition] = value.evolve(status=s)`, so within a single frame `len(conditions)` increases step by step. But because each qualifying call starts from a brand-new empty dict, the global chronological sequence across all frames repeatedly jumps back down from a completed frame to the next frame’s fresh build. That means the overall sequence is not non-decreasing or non-increasing.

## Answer
{"monotonicity":"None"}
